<resources>
    <!-- Display cutout.

         Values taken from the STOCK framework RRO, not from the device as it
         currently runs:
           .build/work/product/overlay/framework-res__auto_generated_rro_product.apk
         That distinction matters. The phone is presently on a third-party ROM
         whose overlay declares the cutout 36dp tall; stock declares 40dp. Reading
         a running device would have baked somebody else's authored value into
         this tree.

         The path is a bounding region, deliberately larger than the hole itself.
         Stock's Transsion-specific resources describe the actual punch-hole as
         centre (360,48)px radius 26px on a 720px-wide panel — a ~52px circle —
         while this rect is 48dp x 40dp (~82 x 68px at density 273). Do not
         "tighten" it to the circle; the extra area is the layout safe zone. -->
    <string name="config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutout" translatable="false">M -24,0 L -24,40 L 24,40 L 24,0 Z @dp</string>

    <!-- Stock leaves the cutout unfilled, so the status bar keeps its normal
         background rather than painting the notch region black. -->
    <bool name="config_fillMainBuiltInDisplayCutout">false</bool>

    <!-- Rounded corners: stock says 25dp. The 44px figure noted earlier came
         from the running device and is the same quantity resolved at density
         273 (25dp * 273/160 = 42.7px); the dp source value is what belongs in
         an overlay, since it stays correct if the density is ever retuned. -->
    <dimen name="rounded_corner_radius">25dp</dimen>

    <!-- Refresh rate. The panel exposes 60 / 90 / 120 Hz (dumpsys display on
         the hardware; 120 is the mode it comes up in). Stock ships NO
         config_defaultPeakRefreshRate override, so this one line is an
         authored choice, not a recovered stock fact: without it AOSP settles
         at 60 and the 90/120 modes are never selected.

         Deliberately no config_defaultRefreshRate — leaving it unset lets the
         framework drop to 60 on static content instead of pinning a floor. -->
    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">120</integer>

    <!-- FINGERPRINT SENSOR DECLARATION.
         Without this there is no fingerprint option in Settings at all, even
         though the HAL is running and registered. Measured on build 59:

           lshal      biometrics.fingerprint@2.1::IBiometricsFingerprint/default
                      registered, pid 1062
           ps         ...fingerprint@2.1-service   running
           pm list features                        android.hardware.fingerprint
           dumpsys biometric                       Sensors: ID(1008) modality 8

         Modality 8 is FACE. So the framework had registered a face sensor and
         no fingerprint one, and Settings hides the enrolment UI when there is
         no fingerprint sensor. The HAL was up the whole time and nothing ever
         asked it anything.

         AOSP ships this array EMPTY, with the example commented out in
         frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml, so every device is
         expected to declare its own. Stock's framework RRO does NOT carry it,
         because stock is Transsion own framework-res which sets it natively,
         and an RRO contains only OVERRIDES of AOSP values. That is why it never
         appeared in the 90 override decode.

         Format is sensorId:modality:strength. 2 is TYPE_FINGERPRINT and
         15 is BIOMETRIC_STRONG (0x000F). This device has a rear capacitive
         sensor served by the HIDL 2.1 HAL, which is the plain 0:2:15 case, with
         no UDFPS properties needed. -->
    <string-array name="config_biometric_sensors" translatable="false">
        <item>0:2:15</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
